        {type:"example", title:"Case Study 1 — The forest permit", question:"Imagine a community that traditionally collects firewood, grazes cattle and gathers fruits from a forest. A new law requires a permit for each activity. What changes?", answer:"The community's daily activities become regulated and potentially more expensive or restricted. People may lose access to resources, become dependent on wage labour or be punished for practices that were previously customary. The policy can therefore become a major livelihood crisis."},
        {type:"example", title:"Case Study 2 — The loan", question:"A cultivator borrows money after a crop failure and cannot repay. Why might the loan affect the family's future?", answer:"Interest can increase the debt. The family may have to sell produce, labour or land to repay. If land is lost, future agricultural income also falls, making the household more vulnerable."},
        {type:"example", title:"Case Study 3 — The outsider", question:"Why might a trader initially appear useful but later be viewed as a diku?", answer:"The trader may provide access to markets and goods, but if unfair prices, debt, manipulation or unequal bargaining develops, the trader may become associated with exploitation and loss of control."},
        {type:"example", title:"Case Study 4 — Golden age", question:"Why can an idealised past motivate a political movement?", answer:"A shared memory of a better order can give people a common identity and a clear contrast with present oppression. It can transform dissatisfaction into a collective political goal."}
