        {type:"paragraph", title:"Must remember", text:"• Tribal communities were diverse; there was no single tribal way of life.\n• Forests supplied food, fuel, fodder, medicine, materials and cultivation resources.\n• Shifting cultivation was suited to certain forest and hill environments.\n• Colonial officials preferred settled cultivation because it was easier to survey and tax.\n• The colonial state wanted timber for railways, ships and construction.\n• Forest laws restricted many customary practices.\n• Tribal chiefs often lost autonomy as they became linked to colonial administration.\n• Moneylenders, traders, landlords and officials could be viewed as dikus when associated with exploitation.\n• Debt and land alienation were major sources of rural distress.\n• The Santhal Rebellion occurred in 1855–56.\n• Sidhu, Kanhu, Chand and Bhairav were major Santhal leaders.\n• The Munda movement was led by Birsa Munda.\n• Birsa combined religious reform with political resistance.\n• His golden age involved restoration of Munda autonomy and freedom from dikus.\n• The Munda Uprising occurred in 1899–1900.\n• Birsa was captured on 3 February 1900.\n• Birsa died in prison on 9 June 1900.\n• Historical images must be read critically because their creators had their own viewpoints."}
